---
/* /privacy: must stay true of the code. Analytics: src/components/Analytics.astro, src/lib/track.ts and the visitor trail (src/lib/trail.ts, src/trail-endpoint.ts). Clarity: src/lib/clarity.ts. Form: src/pages/contact.astro. */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/pages/legal.css';
import Closing from '../components/Closing.astro';

import { site } from '../data/site';
import { plainText, wordSpans } from '../lib/words';

const heading = `What this site <b>collects.</b>`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy | Nixon Creative Studio"
  description="What this site collects when you visit or send the contact form, what I do with it, and who handles it: Cloudflare, Google Analytics and Microsoft Clarity."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1 aria-label={plainText(heading)} set:html={wordSpans(heading)} />
    <p class="lede">
      Short version: a contact form, tools that count visits (Cloudflare, Google Analytics), a
      record of which pages each browser looked at, recordings of how pages are used (Microsoft
      Clarity, with anything typed hidden), and the things any web server logs. No advertising
      trackers. If you have questions, email <a href={site.emailHref}>{site.email}</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="updated">Last updated November 16, 2026</p>
  </header>
  <div class="doc">
    <nav class="toc card" aria-label="On this page">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="#contact-form">Contact form</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#analytics">Analytics</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#server-logs">Server logs</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#cookies">Cookies</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#your-data">Your data</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#changes">Changes</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="contact-form">Contact form</h2>
      <p>
        When you submit the form on the Start a project page, the fields you fill in go to this
        site's own server on Cloudflare. It saves them in a small database there, so an inquiry is
        never lost if an email goes astray, and then emails them to my inbox through Cloudflare. If
        that email fails, a backup service,{' '}
        <a href="https://web3forms.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          Web3Forms
        </a>
        , delivers it instead and does not keep it afterwards. I keep inquiries the same way I'd
        keep any project email, and I'll delete yours from both places if you ask.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fields are your name, email, organization type and what you are hoping to build, plus
        anything you add from the optional ones: who will keep it up to date, a budget range, and
        under "A few optional details" your organization's name, phone, current website, timeline
        and how you heard about me. Four hidden fields go with it, filled only when the link that
        brought you to the form carried them: <code>from</code> (the page or case study you came
        from), <code>tier</code> (a price tier you picked), <code>service</code> (a service you
        picked) and <code>origin_org_type</code> (the kind of organization that link named). They
        tell me where an inquiry started, nothing more. Two more things go with every message, both
        explained under <a href="#analytics">Analytics</a>: the note about how you first found the
        site, and your browser's random visitor number.
      </p>
      <p>
        The form also includes a hidden "honeypot" field that real visitors never fill in. If a bot
        submits the form and trips the honeypot, the message is quietly discarded.
      </p>
      <p>
        To keep spam out, the form also uses{' '}
        <a
          href="https://www.cloudflare.com/turnstile-privacy-policy/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Cloudflare Turnstile
        </a>
        , a check that tells people from bots, usually without asking you to do anything. With your
        message the site saves a few things Cloudflare already knows about the connection: the rough
        country, region and city it seems to come from, the name of the network (your internet or
        phone provider), and your browser's name. It does not save your IP address. The site then
        gives each inquiry a spam score from simple signs, like a name full of numbers or a
        throwaway email address. A message that looks like spam is still kept, just set aside for me
        to check rather than emailed straight to me.
      </p>
      <h2 id="analytics">Analytics</h2>
      <p>
        The site uses two tools that count visits, plus its own record of pages and Microsoft
        Clarity's recordings (both below). I use all of them for the same thing: to see which pages
        are useful, which aren't, and what people are looking for.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a
          href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Cloudflare Web Analytics
        </a>{' '}
        counts page views and basic performance metrics without cookies, without fingerprinting, and
        without sending data to any third-party advertising network.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a
          href="https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/6004245"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Google Analytics 4
        </a>{' '}
        records page views, scrolls, and outbound clicks, and sends them to Google. It sets
        first-party cookies to tell returning visits apart, and it truncates IP addresses before
        storing anything. I do not use it for advertising, I have not linked it to any ad account,
        and I have not turned on Google Signals or any demographic reporting. To opt out everywhere,
        install Google's{' '}
        <a
          href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          browser add-on
        </a>
        ; most tracker-blocking extensions and browsers also stop it.
      </p>
      <p>
        On top of page views, Google Analytics gets a handful of named events, so I can tell which
        parts of the site lead to a conversation: <code>generate_lead</code> (the contact form went
        through), <code>contact_click</code> (an email or phone link),{' '}
        <code>visit_client_site</code> (a link out to a client's live site from a case study) and{' '}
        <code>pricing_view</code> (a pricing section came into view), <code>tool_use</code> (the
        first time you try one of the site's tools, such as the tier picker or the cost receipt),
        and three for the contact form: <code>inquiry_start</code>, <code>inquiry_step</code> (which
        question you reached) and <code>inquiry_error</code> (a send that failed). Each one carries
        only a fixed label, such as where on the page the click was or which client site it went to.
        When the form goes through, <code>generate_lead</code> also carries the options you picked
        from the lists (organization type, budget range, timing, how you heard about me), never
        anything you typed, your name, your email address or the text of a message.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I can tell which channels bring real inquiries, this site keeps one small note in your
        browser's own storage on your first visit: the page you landed on, the name of the site that
        sent you (just the hostname, like google.com) and any campaign tags in the link. It stays on
        your device and is not sent anywhere on its own. If you send the contact form, that note
        goes with your message, along with how many pages you looked at, so I can see how you found
        me. Clearing your browser's site data removes it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those campaign tags are why links I post on Instagram, Facebook, LinkedIn, my email
        signature or a proposal end in something like <code>?utm_source=instagram</code>. They are
        plain labels that say which link you followed; no code runs and nobody else sees them. The
        social sites may add tracking of their own to links you tap there (Facebook and Instagram
        add an <code>fbclid</code> code, for example); that is theirs, covered by their privacy
        policies, and this site does not use it. The site itself loads no advertising trackers: no
        Meta Pixel, no LinkedIn Insight Tag, no ad network tags.
      </p>
      <h3 id="visit-history">Pages you looked at</h3>
      <p>
        This site keeps its own record of visits, on its own server, so I can see what people
        actually read. Your browser gets a random visitor number (<code>ncs-vid</code>), and for
        each page it sends that number with the page's address and title, where the visit came from,
        how long the page was open and in use, how far down you scrolled, and short notes of what
        you used there: the site's tools, the buttons and questions you opened, words you typed into
        the site's search box, and which contact-form question you reached (its name, like "budget",
        never your answer). It never includes anything typed into the contact form. The server adds
        the rough city and region Cloudflare sees and the kind of device (for example "iPhone,
        Safari"), and it does not keep your IP address.
      </p>
      <p>
        On its own that record is anonymous: a number, not a name. If you send the contact form, the
        number goes with your message, so I can see what you looked at before you wrote. The same
        number is passed to Google Analytics and Microsoft Clarity so their records line up with
        this one. Visits that never lead to a message are deleted after 13 months.
      </p>
      <h3 id="recordings">Session recordings</h3>
      <p>
        <a href="https://clarity.microsoft.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          Microsoft Clarity
        </a>{' '}
        records how pages are used: mouse movement, taps, scrolling and clicks, played back as a
        recording and summed up as heatmaps. I use it to find the places where the site confuses
        people. The contact form is hidden from it entirely, and Clarity masks other text you type.
        It is not used for advertising. It does not run at all if your browser sends a Global
        Privacy Control signal, which several browsers and extensions can turn on, and blocking
        clarity.ms in a tracker blocker stops it too.
      </p>
      <h3 id="opt-out">Turning it all off</h3>
      <p>
        To stop all of it in one go, open <a href="/?me=1">this link</a>, or add
        <code>?me=1</code> to the address of any page on the site. That browser then sends nothing
        to Google Analytics, keeps no record of pages and runs no recordings, until you visit with{' '}
        <code>?me=0</code> or clear the site's data. It is the same switch I use on my own devices
        so my visits don't count.
      </p>
      <h2 id="server-logs">Server logs</h2>
      <p>
        The hosting platform (Cloudflare) records standard request data: IP address, user agent,
        requested URL, response code, timestamp. These logs are retained for a short window and used
        for operational purposes like blocking abuse and diagnosing errors. They are not used to
        build profiles of visitors.
      </p>
      <h2 id="cookies">Cookies</h2>
      <p>
        Google Analytics sets a small number of first-party cookies (the <code>_ga</code> family) so
        it can tell a returning visit from a new one. They carry a random identifier, not your name
        or email, and the Analytics section above links the opt-out. The fonts are served from this
        site, so no font service sees your visit. Cloudflare, which hosts the site, may also set a
        short-lived security cookie called <code>cf_clearance</code> when it checks that a visit
        comes from a real browser rather than a bot; it is used only for that check. Nothing else on
        the site sets cookies, apart from Microsoft Clarity's two first-party cookies (
        <code>_clck</code> and <code>_clsk</code>), which hold a random identifier so one visit's
        recording hangs together. If a future change adds more third-party content, this page will
        say so first.
      </p>
      <p>
        Separate from cookies, the site keeps a few small choices in your browser's own storage.
        They stay on your device and are not sent anywhere on their own. <code>ncs-theme</code> is
        light or dark, <code>ncs-sound</code> is whether sound is on, and <code>ncs-slash</code> is
        whether the / key opens the search. While you fill in the contact form,
        <code>ncs-contact-draft</code> keeps a draft of your answers so nothing is lost if the page
        closes; it is cleared once the form is sent. A few short notes last only as long as the tab
        is open, so one page can pick up where the last left off: <code>ncs-bp</code> (the opening
        drawing has already played), <code>ncs-hero</code> and <code>ncs-tier-morph</code> (the
        window or price card you followed), <code>ncs-tier</code> (a tier you chose on the Prices
        page), <code>ncs-mark-wave</code> (the name in the top line has already waved) and the page
        count mentioned above.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three more are different, because they do leave your device. <code>ncs-vid</code> is the
        visitor number described under Pages you looked at, sent with each page and with the contact
        form; <code>ncs-visit</code> (for as long as the tab is open) says which visit a page
        belongs to and is sent with it; and <code>ncs-me</code> is the off switch described under
        Turning it all off, which is never sent anywhere. Clearing your browser's site data removes
        all of them.
      </p>
      <h2 id="your-data">Your data</h2>
      <p>
        If you've sent a project inquiry and want me to delete it from my inbox, email{' '}
        <a href={site.emailHref}>{site.email}</a> with the email address you used to send it. I'll
        confirm when it's deleted. That includes the record of pages you looked at before you wrote,
        and the matching Clarity recordings.
      </p>
      <h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If what the site collects changes, I'll update this page and bump the "Last updated" date at
        the top. There's no separate notification list for privacy updates.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <Closing slot="end" />
</BaseLayout>
